ort":"xhigh","userType":"external","entrypoint":"claude-desktop","cwd":"/Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern","sessionId":"e6419d94-65c2-4a67-8a07-67ae7e574ad6","version":"2.1.321","gitBranch":"formal/spec2-m4","slug":"replicated-napping-garden"}
{"parentUuid":"de1a3814-458f-4b68-b83f-e31dda1aadbb","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"0a3077be-f3d6-44b7-a169-c1486d08c4f2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_0196Pp4KVGToTKaEJWeeNq2y","type":"tool_result","content":"## C17. Reference Unlock — `unlock` **[WC5 MODIFIED — D-C1]**\n\n`unlock` is a candidate derived library operation.\n\n**Abstract preconditions**\n\n1. A live entry has `grant = some g`.\n2. `g` is active and legally releasable under O3.\n3. No active descendant or surviving validity anchor prevents its release.\n4. The acting frame has the applicable controller-access right, with effective mode present on `ControlOf(entry)` (Spec 1 §68.2).\n\n**Proposed outcomes (D-C1)**\n\n- **`ok`** if all applicable preconditions hold and the coherent effect below is completed.\n- **`reject`** if any required entry, register, authority, dependency, O3-release, or controller-access condition fails, including a recorded `g` that has become inactive. Contention does not convert such a failure into temporary non-enablement.\n\nThis candidate defines no waiting outcome for `unlock` on a failed C17 precondition. A physical implementation may impose its own internal progress protocol, but it may not report an invalid request as a successful abstract unlock.\n\n**Abstract effect**\n\nThe grant is released according to O3, the registry records `grant = none`, and the controller's logical synchronized state is made available as one coherent library transition.\n\n**Realization obligation, not an assumed abstract precondition:** The controller update must satisfy `K.SharedMutable` and the required event, atomicity and ordering discipline. The physical protocol and its correspondence are specified provisionally in C41, C48 and C49 and remain under `OB-C05`, rather than being treated as proven by the abstract transition.\n\n-----\n## C21. Synchronization Invariant — `SyncInv`\n\nFor the reference mutex abstraction, the following state properties must be preserved over all **live** entries, except the bound-identity property in item 6, which applies to every entry:\n\n1. **Guarded authority:** `guarded` is active, has own mode `Modify`, and is held by a usable place.\n2. **Grant record:** If `grant = some g`, then the recorded authority exists and `origin(g) = guarded` and `footprint(g) = footprint(guarded)`. `g` need not be active; the invariant must handle legal release and reanchoring without silently weakening these equalities.\n3. **Controller validity:** `ControlOf(entry)` identifies an existing usable **leaf** associated with the holder `slot` and its structural mutex parent `M`.\n4. **Controller uniqueness:** Distinct live entries have distinct `ControlOf` leaves; the guarded-identity uniqueness guard is also respected. The same controller cannot be registered twice under different guarded identities.\n5. **`GrantSubtree`:** If `grant = some g` and `g` is active, every active descendant of `guarded` covering a protected leaf is either `g` itself or a descendant of `g`. This is a distinct invariant, necessary to restore grant availability after legal `unlock`.\n6. **`RegBound`:** Every registered `guarded` identity denotes an existing authority identity, even when its entry is no longer live. This prevents a dormant entry from becoming live merely when a fresh authority is created with its identity.\n7. **Structural initialization:** For each live entry, every structural leaf of the place `slot` holding `guarded` is `Init`, and the associated controller leaf `ctl = ControlOf(entry)` is `Init`. This does not impose initialization of storage merely owned by `M` but outside its structural footprint. Preservation by every admitted transition is an obligation to prove, not an assumed precondition.\n\n`SyncInv` is not a claim that every physical controller update is already modeled. Controller-state and event correspondence remain obligations of the library realization in C25 and `OB-C05`.\n\nThe live-entry invariants must remain valid when a recorded grant becomes inactive without `unlock`.\n\n## C22. Grant Discipline and Safe Abstraction Encapsulation **[WC5 MODIFIED — D-C6 (item 4); WC5.1 (item 6)]**\n\nThe reference abstraction requires **`GrantDiscipline`**:\n\n```\nlive(entry) ∧ entry.grant = none\n⇒ no active descendant of entry.guarded\n   covers any leaf of footprint(entry.guarded)\n```\n\nIt is not implied by structural exclusivity I1 alone. Its proof must use an explicit encapsulation condition on the **allowed traces of the safe abstraction** (`EncapsulatedTrace` in the exploratory model). This is an abstraction contract, not a new restriction of the inherited kernel O1–O16.\n\nFor each live synchronization entry, that contract has the following **six requirements** **[WC5.1]**:\n\n1. **Ordinary derivation:** the safe abstraction does not expose an inherited `derive` or `split` operation with `source = guarded`. No independent ordinary derivative may be issued directly from `guarded` while the entry is live.\n2. **Synchronized derivation:** the safe abstraction does not expose an independent, raw `deriveSync` of `guarded`. Such derivation is available only inside the validated `acquire` operation.\n3. **Protected structural transfers:** the safe abstraction does not expose an independent `transfer(source, destination)` when `source` is (a) the place `slot` directly holding `guarded`, (b) any **strict structural subplace** of `slot`, or (c) the associated controller leaf `ctl`. For a by-value `spawn` with multiple transfers, this restriction is evaluated for **each** transfer source against the state at the start of its own transfer phase, as defined in C5.2. A transfer of the complete mutex value `M`, or of a structurally enclosing value, remains eligible for O4 under **O4's own unchanged preconditions**. Preservation of `SyncInv`, including positional association, initialization and controller uniqueness, by every eligible transfer is a **proof obligation**, not an additional authorization precondition. This rule restricts the safe interface, not the kernel transition O4.\n4. **Controller-access protocol (WC5 D-C6):** the safe abstraction does not expose ordinary `read3` of `ControlOf(entry)`, even when its user holds `Observe` for the controller as required by C14.3. It also does not expose ordinary `write3` or out-of-protocol `writeShared` mutations of that controller capable of invalidating its synchronization protocol. Valid internal atomic controller operations performed by `acquire`, `unlock`, or authorized recovery remain part of their protocol, not arbitrary public primitive access. Physical enforcement and correspondence belong to Specs 4/10 and `OB-C05`; an ordinary kernel read or write may independently return `ok` under the unchanged O9/O10, but is not thereby legal through the safe mutex interface.\n5. **Protected end-of-use:** while the entry is live, the safe abstraction does not expose an inherited `endUse(place)` when `place` is (a) the controller leaf `ctl` or (b) any **strict structural subplace** of `slot`. `endUse(slot)`, `endUse(M)` and operations on structural enclosing values retain their unmodified kernel O12 preconditions and effects; their validity depends on those rules, including the release or invalidation of any required authorities. This restriction does not change O12 or prohibit every legal termination of the mutex value.\n\n6. **Protected holder-chain justification [WC5.1]:** for every ordinary exercisability judgment under Spec 1 §43 (`ExercisableAt`) or right over a place (`HasRight`) on which an admitted step depends, there must exist a valid justification whose **entire authority-holder chain excludes the `guarded` authority of every live synchronization entry**. The requirement applies to the actual right relied on by the step, including the source, target, owner, holder, controller and administration rights where relevant, rather than only the authority explicitly passed as an argument. It includes rights checked in intermediate or resulting states of a composite step whenever the inherited precondition checks them there. A different valid chain that does not pass through a live `guarded` suffices. Ordinary access through a legitimate acquired grant is permitted: the grant's justification is through its rightful frame or holder chain, and the grant itself is not excluded merely because its origin is `guarded`. This is **safe-abstraction trace encapsulation**, not a change to any inherited Spec 1 precondition.\n\n   **`resetLock` exception [WC5.1]:** C18.1 deliberately checks `ExercisableAt(entry.guarded, Modify)` for the entry being recovered. Its **subject** `entry.guarded` is exempt from the no-live-`guarded` test for that recovery check alone. The right on the holding `slot` that justifies this exercise must nevertheless have a valid holder chain containing **no other** live entry's `guarded`. This exception neither admits arbitrary access through `entry.guarded` nor relaxes C18.2–C18.3, controller/recovery ordering, or any other ordinary access rule.\n\n   **Verification boundary [WC5.1]:** `ChainExcl` checks the direct-exercise fragment (`derive`, `split`, `read3`, `write3`, `writeShared`), and `PlaceExcl` checks selected `HasRight` premises; their conjunction is represented exploratorily by `Encap5R`. Both guard-exclusion witnesses and the normal mutex, arena and whole-mutex-transfer witnesses have been checked in that fragment. The complete coverage of all rights and composite phases, including the `resetLock` exception, remains `OB-C28`. A kernel-`ok` step that violates this requirement is excluded by the safe abstraction but does not alter its kernel outcome.\n\nRequirements 1–6 all constrain the allowed traces of the safe abstraction **[WC5.1]**. In particular, requirement 4 excludes ordinary `read3(ControlOf(entry))` from `EncapsulatedTrace` as required by C51, while preserving O9 as a kernel operation. Requirement 6 also excludes accesses and other rights justified through a live `guarded` without the relevant grant. Their enforcement must follow from encapsulation and the public interface, rather than hidden runtime constraints or undisclosed hypotheses on the kernel. Preservation of `SyncInv` and `GrantDiscipline` under these explicit restrictions is the proof obligation stated in C23.\n\nRequirement 4 is **also** a physical realization/interface obligation; its enforcement is not falsely claimed to follow merely from the authority-only predicate `GrantDiscipline` or the abstract machine `S_C`.\nShell cwd was reset to /Users/guygouri/Devlab/ernlang/ern","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"059d18eb-58b3-4813-8ecc-ec2c09673b70","timestamp":"2026-11-12T07:08:44.496Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"## C17. Reference Unlock — `unlock` **[WC5 MODIFIED — D-C1]**\n\n`unlock` is a candidate derived library operation.\n\n**Abstract preconditions**\n\n1. A live entry has `grant = some g`.\n2. `g` is active and legally releasable under O3.\n3. No active descendant or surviving validity anchor prevents its release.\n4. The acting frame has the applicable controller-access right, with effective mode present on `ControlOf(entry)` (Spec 1 §68.2).\n\n**Proposed outcomes (D-C1)**\n\n- **`ok`** if all applicable preconditions hold and the coherent effect below is completed.\n- **`reject`** if any required entry, register, authority, dependency, O3-release, or controller-access condition fails, including a recorded `g` that has become inactive. Contention does not convert such a failure into temporary non-enablement.\n\nThis candidate defines no waiting outcome for `unlock` on a failed C17 precondition. A physical implementation may impose its own internal progress protocol, but it may not report an invalid request as a successful abstract unlock.\n\n**Abstract effect**\n\nThe grant is released according to O3, the registry records `grant = none`, and the controller's logical synchronized state is made available