 place `slot` holding `guarded` is `Init`, and the associated controller leaf `ctl = ControlOf(entry)` is `Init`. This does not impose initialization of storage merely owned by `M` but outside its structural footprint. Preservation by every admitted transition is an obligation to prove, not an assumed precondition.\n\n`SyncInv` is not a claim that every physical controller update is already modeled. Controller-state and event correspondence remain obligations of the library realization in C25 and `OB-C05`.\n\nThe live-entry invariants must remain valid when a recorded grant becomes inactive without `unlock`.\n\n## C22. Grant Discipline and Safe Abstraction Encapsulation **[WC5 MODIFIED — D-C6 (item 4); WC5.1 (item 6)]**\n\nThe reference abstraction requires **`GrantDiscipline`**:\n\n```\nlive(entry) ∧ entry.grant = none\n⇒ no active descendant of entry.guarded\n   covers any leaf of footprint(entry.guarded)\n```\n\nIt is not implied by structural exclusivity I1 alone. Its proof must use an explicit encapsulation condition on the **allowed traces of the safe abstraction** (`EncapsulatedTrace` in the exploratory model). This is an abstraction contract, not a new restriction of the inherited kernel O1–O16.\n\nFor each live synchronization entry, that contract has the following **six requirements** **[WC5.1]**:\n\n1. **Ordinary derivation:** the safe abstraction does not expose an inherited `derive` or `split` operation with `source = guarded`. No independent ordinary derivative may be issued directly from `guarded` while the entry is live.\n2. **Synchronized derivation:** the safe abstraction does not expose an independent, raw `deriveSync` of `guarded`. Such derivation is available only inside the validated `acquire` operation.\n3. **Protected structural transfers:** the safe abstraction does not expose an independent `transfer(source, destination)` when `source` is (a) the place `slot` directly holding `guarded`, (b) any **strict structural subplace** of `slot`, or (c) the associated controller leaf `ctl`. For a by-value `spawn` with multiple transfers, this restriction is evaluated for **each** transfer source against the state at the start of its own transfer phase, as defined in C5.2. A transfer of the complete mutex value `M`, or of a structurally enclosing value, remains eligible for O4 under **O4's own unchanged preconditions**. Preservation of `SyncInv`, including positional association, initialization and controller uniqueness, by every eligible transfer is a **proof obligation**, not an additional authorization precondition. This rule restricts the safe interface, not the kernel transition O4.\n4. **Controller-access protocol (WC5 D-C6):** the safe abstraction does not expose ordinary `read3` of `ControlOf(entry)`, even when its user holds `Observe` for the controller as required by C14.3. It also does not expose ordinary `write3` or out-of-protocol `writeShared` mutations of that controller capable of invalidating its synchronization protocol. Valid internal atomic controller operations performed by `acquire`, `unlock`, or authorized recovery remain part of their protocol, not arbitrary public primitive access. Physical enforcement and correspondence belong to Specs 4/10 and `OB-C05`; an ordinary kernel read or write may independently return `ok` under the unchanged O9/O10, but is not thereby legal through the safe mutex interface.\n5. **Protected end-of-use:** while the entry is live, the safe abstraction does not expose an inherited `endUse(place)` when `place` is (a) the controller leaf `ctl` or (b) any **strict structural subplace** of `slot`. `endUse(slot)`, `endUse(M)` and operations on structural enclosing values retain their unmodified kernel O12 preconditions and effects; their validity depends on those rules, including the release or invalidation of any required authorities. This restriction does not change O12 or prohibit every legal termination of the mutex value.\n\n6. **Protected holder-chain justification [WC5.1]:** for every ordinary exercisability judgment under Spec 1 §43 (`ExercisableAt`) or right over a place (`HasRight`) on which an admitted step depends, there must exist a valid justification whose **entire authority-holder chain excludes the `guarded` authority of every live synchronization entry**. The requirement applies to the actual right relied on by the step, including the source, target, owner, holder, controller and administration rights where relevant, rather than only the authority explicitly passed as an argument. It includes rights checked in intermediate or resulting states of a composite step whenever the inherited precondition checks them there. A different valid chain that does not pass through a live `guarded` suffices. Ordinary access through a legitimate acquired grant is permitted: the grant's justification is through its rightful frame or holder chain, and the grant itself is not excluded merely because its origin is `guarded`. This is **safe-abstraction trace encapsulation**, not a change to any inherited Spec 1 precondition.\n\n   **`resetLock` exception [WC5.1]:** C18.1 deliberately checks `ExercisableAt(entry.guarded, Modify)` for the entry being recovered. Its **subject** `entry.guarded` is exempt from the no-live-`guarded` test for that recovery check alone. The right on the holding `slot` that justifies this exercise must nevertheless have a valid holder chain containing **no other** live entry's `guarded`. This exception neither admits arbitrary access through `entry.guarded` nor relaxes C18.2–C18.3, controller/recovery ordering, or any other ordinary access rule.\n\n   **Verification boundary [WC5.1]:** `ChainExcl` checks the direct-exercise fragment (`derive`, `split`, `read3`, `write3`, `writeShared`), and `PlaceExcl` checks selected `HasRight` premises; their conjunction is represented exploratorily by `Encap5R`. Both guard-exclusion witnesses and the normal mutex, arena and whole-mutex-transfer witnesses have been checked 